All six images are visibly accessible: aerial and front views of the hall, bakery/workcourt, and forge.

**Hall — provisional acceptance, with a roof-junction check.**
- The main roof and lower front roof look closed across their visible surfaces. The porch roof’s rear connection to the lower roof is obscured; that junction remains unresolved in these views.
- Porch posts, triangular framing, and diagonal braces give the entrance a credible support structure. No clearly floating major beam is visible.
- The large, mostly blank timber side wall makes the aerial silhouette somewhat boxy. A window or more deliberate framing rhythm could break up that surface.
- The entrance reads as open, though the furniture visible behind it makes its intended approach less visually clear. The building reads as a communal hall.

**Bakery/workcourt — provisional acceptance; clarify the central connection.**
- Both pitched roofs look coherent, with no obvious holes on the visible slopes. Their rear closure cannot be assessed.
- The narrow central opening and horizontal lintel read as a connecting passage. The exposed roof-edge elements around it make the junction feel less resolved than the two principal bays.
- The broad front openings and masonry piers communicate support; no unmistakably unsupported major beam appears.
- Bread, oven, counters, and tools make the craft purpose readable. The two distinct roof volumes prevent excessive boxiness.
- Both work bays visibly open toward the front. This establishes visual access only.

**Forge — revise the upper mass; craft purpose already works.**
- The anvil, hearth, tools, and water barrel communicate smithing immediately.
- The tall rectangular body above the hearth has a large blank cream face. Its proportions make it read more like a wall panel supporting a chimney than a shaped forge hood. Tapering or stepping this mass would strengthen the architecture.
- The chimney’s dark top looks capped; a visible outlet or clearly raised rain cap would better explain exhaust.
- The bright orange fire reads as a solid polygonal lump.
- A small dark fragment appears detached near the lower left in the front view; inspect it.

These are provisional design observations, not final-gate, live-world, locomotion, collision, or Bill approval.
